```

catchThrowableOfType signature: `catchThrowableOfType(Class<T>, ThrowingCallable)` — and ThrowingCallable.call throws Throwable, so I can pass `call::call`? Actually `catchThrowableOfType(ApiException.class, call)` directly — call is a ThrowingCallable. If call throws ApiException, it's captured. If it throws something else, catchThrowableOfType... it propagates? catchThrowableOfType catches Throwable and returns it if instance of type, else... Actually AssertJ's catchThrowableOfType: "if the raised exception is not of the specified type, it is rethrown"? Let me recall: `catchThrowableOfType(ThrowingCallable, Class)` returns null if no exception; if exception of different type is thrown, it... I believe it throws a ClassCastException or assertion error? Safer to write my own:

```java
private static ApiException apiException(ThrowingCallable call, HttpStatus status) {
    try {
        call.call();
    } catch (ApiException e) {
        assertThat(e.status()).isEqualTo(status);
